   "body": {
    "type": "TextualBody",
    "value": "December 6, 1913",
    "format": "text/plain"
   },
   "target": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7#xywh=156,172,361,43"
  },
  {
   "id": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7/text/r1",
   "type": "Annotation",
   "motivation": "supplementing",
   "body": {
    "type": "TextualBody",
    "value": "Constitutional Amendment, now before it; and be it further Resolved, That the meeting urges the Rules Committee of the House of Representatives to report favorably on the resolution creating a standing committee on Woman Suffrage in the House. The resolutions were enthusiastically carried by the whole meeting with but two dissenting votes.",
    "format": "text/plain"
   },
   "target": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7#xywh=115,297,697,491"
  },
  {
   "id": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7/text/r2",
   "type": "Annotation",
   "motivation": "supplementing",
   "body": {
    "type": "TextualBody",
    "value": "The case for Woman Suffrage was eloquently stated at this meeting by Miss Jane Addams, Miss Margaret Hinchey, laundry worker; Miss Rose Winslow, weaver; Miss Mary Anderson, member of the National Sock and Shoe Maker's Union, and the distinguished woman Senator, Mrs. Helen Ring Robinson.",
    "format": "text/plain"
   },
   "target": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7#xywh=116,789,695,396"
  },
  {
   "id": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7/text/r3",
   "type": "Annotation",
   "motivation": "supplementing",
   "body": {
    "type": "TextualBody",
    "value": "This Sunday meeting was the first of a series of packed evening meetings. On Monday evening, Mrs. Grace Wilbur Trout, Mrs. Medill McCormick, Mrs. Sherman Booth, Mrs. Ella S. Stewart, and Mrs. Catherine Waugh McCulloch of Chicago, told of the battle for Suffrage in Illinois, and the steps by which victory was achieved. Dr. Anna Howard Shaw devoted an impassioned and powerful address to the subject of Federal work for Woman Suffrage. Miss Lucy Burns, vice-chairman of the Congressional Committee, stated clearly and briefly the purpose of the meeting as follows",
    "format": "text/plain"
   },
   "target": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7#xywh=121,1188,693,745"
  },
  {
   "id": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7/text/r4",
   "type": "Annotation",
   "motivation": "supplementing",
   "body": {
    "type": "TextualBody",
    "value": "“The National Woman Suffrage Association is assembled in Washington to ask the Democratic party to enfranchise the women of America.",
    "format": "text/plain"
   },
   "target": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7#xywh=126,1937,690,217"
  },
  {
   "id": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7/text/r5",
   "type": "Annotation",
   "motivation": "supplementing",
   "body": {
    "type": "TextualBody",
    "value": "“Rarely in the history of the country has a party been more powerful than the Democratic party is today. It controls the executive offices, the Senate, and more than two-thirds of the members of the House of Representatives. It is in a position to give us effective and immediate help. “We ask the Democrats to take action now. Those who hold power are responsible to the country for",
    "format": "text/plain"
   },
   "target": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7#xywh=127,2156,694,529"
  },
  {
   "id": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7/text/r6",
   "type": "Annotation",
   "motivation": "supplementing",
   "body": {
    "type": "TextualBody",
    "value": "Ss",
    "format": "text/plain"
   },
   "target": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7#xywh=203,2809,550,138"
  },
  {
   "id": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7/text/r7",
   "type": "Annotation",
   "motivation": "supplementing",
   "body": {
    "type": "TextualBody",
    "value": "A CORDIAL INVITATION I1S EXTENDED TO THE DELEGATES AND VISITORS TO THIS CON- VENTION THIS Shopping Headquarters",
    "format": "text/plain"
   },
   "target": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7#xywh=162,2989,640,310"
  },
  {
   "id": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7/text/r8",
   "type": "Annotation",
   "motivation": "supplementing",
   "body": {
    "type": "TextualBody",
    "value": "Full and complete stocks in the following lines of merchandise facilitate shopping— Women's and Children's Outfittings of all kinds Full assortments of Yard Goods Books and Stationery Toys and Games Art Goods, Housefurnishings, Upholsteries, Trunks, Leather Goods, Jewelry, Toilet Goods, Umbrellas, Candies and many other lines. THE ONLY DEP ARTMENT STORE ON PENNSYLVANIA AVENUE",
    "format": "text/plain"
   },
   "target": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7#xywh=154,3343,665,705"
  },
  {
   "id": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7/text/r9",
   "type": "Annotation",
   "motivation": "supplementing",
   "body": {
    "type": "TextualBody",
    "value": "THE SUFFRAGIST",
    "format": "text/plain"
   },
   "target": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7#xywh=1288,161,553,49"
  },
  {
   "id": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7/text/r10",
   "type": "Annotation",
   "motivation": "supplementing",
   "body": {
    "type": "TextualBody",
    "value": "the use of it. They are responsible not only for what they do, but for what they do not do. Imaction establishes just as clear a record as does a policy of open hostility.",
    "format": "text/plain"
   },
   "target": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7#xywh=854,210,709,310"
  },
  {
   "id": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7/text/r11",
   "type": "Annotation",
   "motivation": "supplementing",
   "body": {
    "type": "TextualBody",
    "value": "“We have in our hands today not only the weapon of a just cause; we have the support of ten enfranchised States. More than 3,600,000 women have a vote in Presidential elections. Itis unthinkable that a nationalGovernment which represents women and which appeals periodically to the suffrage of women, should ignore the issue of their right to political freedom.",
    "format": "text/plain"
   },
   "target": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7#xywh=856,520,700,485"
  },
  {
   "id": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7/text/r12",
   "type": "Annotation",
   "motivation": "supplementing",
   "body": {
    "type": "TextualBody",
    "value": "“We can not wait until after the passage of scheduled administration reforms. These reforms, which affect women, should not be enacted without the consent of women. Congress is free to take action on our question in the present session. We ask the Administration to support the Woman Suffrage Amendment in Congress with its whole strength.”",
    "format": "text/plain"
   },
   "target": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7#xywh=857,1009,695,482"
  },
  {
   "id": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7/text/r13",
   "type": "Annotation",
   "motivation": "supplementing",
   "body": {
    "type": "TextualBody",
    "value": "Other propaganda meetings were held on Tuesday, Wednesday, and Thursday evenings. On Tuesday, the program was arranged by Mrs. Joseph Tilton Bowen, of Chicago. The subject of the evening was “The Position of Women Before the Law.\" The speakers on Wednesday evening were Mr. James Lees Laidlaw, president of the National Men's League for Woman Suffrage, Representative Victor Murdock, of Kansas; Representative Frank W. Mondell, of Wyoming; Mr. Gifford Pinchot; Representative William Kent, of California; Representative James W. Bryan, of Washington, Mr. Charles Edward Russell, and Judge John S. Crosby. On Thursday evening, Mrs. Carrie Chapman Catt spoke with Miss Harriet May Mills and Mrs. Thomas Hepburn.",
    "format": "text/plain"
   },
   "target": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7#xywh=862,1500,697,1005"
  },
  {
   "id": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7/text/r14",
   "type": "Annotation",
   "motivation": "supplementing",
   "body": {
    "type": "TextualBody",
    "value": "During the course of the Convention, over $15,000 was raised by subscription for the work of the National Association. The Con- DEWEY HOTEL t4th and L Streets N. W. WASHINGTON, D. C.",
    "format": "text/plain"
   },
   "target": "https://nealcaren.github.io/paperpress-demo/iiif/suffragist/_undated/suffragist01cong_2/canvas/p7#xywh=854,2510,709,523"
  },
